1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,444
This is Home APM — my Track
3 project for the Agents of

2
00:00:04,494 --> 00:00:05,874
SigNoz hackathon.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,186
Over two million homes run on
Home Assistant automations —

4
00:00:10,236 --> 00:00:13,254
but when one misbehaves,
there's no way to see why. Its

5
00:00:13,304 --> 00:00:15,950
built-in trace view
is famously unreadable.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,934
Home APM bridges Home
Assistant's hidden internal traces

7
00:00:18,984 --> 00:00:22,078
into OpenTelemetry, so every
automation run becomes a flame

8
00:00:22,128 --> 00:00:25,274
graph in SigNoz — with logs,
metrics, dashboards, and alerts

9
00:00:25,324 --> 00:00:26,050
to match.

10
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,148
It installs with one command.
Here's what it found in my

11
00:00:29,198 --> 00:00:30,881
house on the first night.

12
00:00:32,433 --> 00:00:35,486
My hallway lights turned on
at three in the morning. And I

13
00:00:35,536 --> 00:00:36,692
had no idea why.

14
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:41,006
Home Assistant does record
a trace. But this is what it

15
00:00:41,056 --> 00:00:44,439
gives you: cryptic node paths,
a run that says it finished

16
00:00:44,489 --> 00:00:47,690
in zero seconds, and it
keeps only the last five.

17
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:53,019
So it couldn't actually
tell me what happened.

18
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:55,456
So I gave my house an APM.

19
00:00:55,506 --> 00:00:58,411
A sidecar turns every
automation run into a real

20
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:01,798
OpenTelemetry trace, and
sends it to SigNoz. Four saved

21
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:05,308
views, one per kind of failure.
Let's open the three a.m.

22
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:06,150
mystery.

23
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:10,835
There it is. A sun-and-time
trigger fired, a choose branch

24
00:01:10,885 --> 00:01:14,063
was taken, and its condition
passed silently, turning the

25
00:01:14,113 --> 00:01:17,234
hallway lights on. The branch
Home Assistant would never

26
00:01:17,284 --> 00:01:19,510
show me is now a
span I can click.

27
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:25,295
And the log line and the
trace are one click apart.

28
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:32,122
The bridge narrates itself:
every converted-run log carries

29
00:01:32,172 --> 00:01:35,210
the trace id it produced.
Logs and traces, natively

30
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,226
correlated.

31
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,673
Next mystery: my morning
routine felt slow.

32
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:50,190
One span explains all of it:
a wait-for-trigger that sat

33
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:53,529
idle for fifty-two seconds.
The villain is impossible to

34
00:01:53,579 --> 00:01:54,178
miss.

35
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,640
This is the part every naive
trace reader gets wrong.

36
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,757
A real tracer. The parallel
block draws as overlapping bars,

37
00:02:08,807 --> 00:02:12,148
one branch clearly slower.
The repeat loop stacks one span

38
00:02:12,198 --> 00:02:15,597
per iteration. And a template
divides by zero, so it lights

39
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:17,291
up as a red error span.

40
00:02:18,267 --> 00:02:20,819
You don't even have
to read a flame graph.

41
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:24,543
Ask in plain English. Through
the SigNoz MCP server, it

42
00:02:24,593 --> 00:02:27,677
finds the run, reads the
span tree, and answers in one

43
00:02:27,727 --> 00:02:31,102
sentence: the silent branch,
the fifty-two-second wait, the

44
00:02:31,152 --> 00:02:34,469
divide-by-zero, each with the
exact trace id and a link to

45
00:02:34,519 --> 00:02:35,748
the flame graph.

46
00:02:44,467 --> 00:02:46,915
And there's a board
my partner could read.

47
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:54,384
Panels titled as questions:
how often are my automations

48
00:02:54,434 --> 00:02:57,956
running, which are slowest, is
anything failing, plus a room

49
00:02:58,006 --> 00:03:00,688
selector that refocuses
the whole board.

50
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,115
Every domain your automations
touch, drawn as a service map

51
00:03:14,165 --> 00:03:16,339
of your house, with
the failing path to

52
00:03:16,389 --> 00:03:18,914
persistent-notification
flagged in red.

53
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:22,957
And when something
breaks, it says so.

54
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:29,014
A traces-based rule, Automation
failing, is firing on those

55
00:03:29,064 --> 00:03:30,068
error spans.

56
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,516
And the loop closes: the SigNoz
alert lands back inside Home

57
00:03:41,566 --> 00:03:45,117
Assistant as a notification,
right where you'd look for it.

58
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:48,625
Home Assistant always had
traces. It just never let anyone

59
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:49,519
see them.

60
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:53,099
So give your house an APM. One
command, and the dashboard's

61
00:03:53,149 --> 00:03:54,187
already live.
